      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1786898212308,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "3) Optimize top K queries",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "3) Optimize top K queries",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "dWJfaZBwV5ixJEE56xEg_",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 872.890625,
      "y": 1526.734375,
      "width": 733.022705078125,
      "height": 900,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bvc",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1013093239,
      "version": 492,
      "versionNonce": 1952655481,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1786898662771,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- Eg: Let's say we need to perform top-K queries in the last 5 hours,\ncalculation can be heavy, Parallel can make thing faster but it comes with\ncost & capacity. \n- #1 Solution: Maintain aggregates for each window in DB\n1) Our Flink jobs will continue to aggregate, at hour grain, the views for\neach video.\n2) Whenever a new hour has passed and we're ready to write to our\ndatabase, we'll make updates to our window tables (e.g.\nVideoViewsLastHour) rather than writing to the hour-grain VideoViews\ntable.\n\n==> There are many ways to optimize these bulk operations in Postgres.\nWe can use unlogged tables to avoid writing to the WAL. We can also delay\nfsync to the end of the transaction to further improve performance.\nChoosing the right batch size will be important here as well. There\n\n\n- #2 Solution: Do aggregation in memory with Flink\nBy maintaining state inside of our Flink jobs, we can get rid of the\nPostgres database entirely. We can also have the top-K calculations\nperformed natively in Flink, meaning we write the output directly to our\nCache at the conclusion of every minute rather than requiring a top-K\ncron.\n\n1) rolling window aggregator is keeping, in memory, the count of views for\neach window for each video. When the window is complete, it emits a\nVideoWindowCount object which contains the window and the count of\nviews for each video in that window.\n2) top-k aggregator is keeping, in memory (likely using a heap), the top K\nvideos for a given window. It receives the VideoWindowCounts and emits a\nTopKResult object which contains the window and the top K videos for\nthat window.\n3) Finally, that TopKResult is written to Redis using a sink.\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- Eg: Let's say we need to perform top-K queries in the last 5 hours, calculation can be heavy, Parallel can make thing faster but it comes with cost & capacity. \n- #1 Solution: Maintain aggregates for each window in DB\n1) Our Flink jobs will continue to aggregate, at hour grain, the views for each video.\n2) Whenever a new hour has passed and we're ready to write to our database, we'll make updates to our window tables (e.g. VideoViewsLastHour) rather than writing to the hour-grain VideoViews table.\n\n==> There are many ways to optimize these bulk operations in Postgres. We can use unlogged tables to avoid writing to the WAL. We can also delay fsync to the end of the transaction to further improve performance. Choosing the right batch size will be important here as well. There\n\n\n- #2 Solution: Do aggregation in memory with Flink\nBy maintaining state inside of our Flink jobs, we can get rid of the Postgres database entirely. We can also have the top-K calculations performed natively in Flink, meaning we write the output directly to our Cache at the conclusion of every minute rather than requiring a top-K cron.\n\n1) rolling window aggregator is keeping, in memory, the count of views for each window for each video. When the window is complete, it emits a VideoWindowCount object which contains the window and the count of views for each video in that window.\n2) top-k aggregator is keeping, in memory (likely using a heap), the top K videos for a given window. It receives the VideoWindowCounts and emits a TopKResult object which contains the window and the top K videos for that window.\n3) Finally, that TopKResult is written to Redis using a sink.\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
